{
  "result": "**The 2 a.m. Laundromat**\n\nIt's 2:07 a.m. and I'm watching my laundry go around in circles.\n\nNot metaphorically. Literally. The big industrial washer on the end has a round window, and my clothes are tumbling in there like they're caught in a very silly, soapy orbit. A blue sweater, two actual socks and one mysterious lonely sock, a t-shirt that has survived approximately one thousand washes and now has the structural integrity of tissue paper. They rise, they fall, they rise again. I could watch this for hours, which is convenient, because I apparently have hours.\n\nI'm here at 2 a.m. because of insomnia, and also because on Tuesday I received a masterclass in adulting: I ran out of clean underwear. This is the glamorous reality nobody warns you about when you move out. They teach you how to pay rent and file taxes, but nobody teaches you the ancient, universal law: you will always, inevitably, need to do laundry at the worst possible time.\n\nThe laundromat on 4th Street never closes. It smells like hot metal and fake lemons and someone else's dryer sheets. The fluorescent lights buzz in a key I can feel in my teeth. One of the dryers has a handwritten sign taped to it: \"EATS QUARTERS DO NOT USE.\" The dryer next to it has a sign that says \"ONLY EATS QUARTERS.\" They're in quote-unquote a committed relationship.\n\nThere's a man asleep in the orange plastic chair by the window, a baseball cap pulled down over his eyes, a duffel bag of clean clothes hugged to his chest like a teddy bear. There's a woman folding fitted sheets with the focus of a surgeon. She is winning at life in a way I do not understand. Sheet folding is a skill I have come to accept I will die without.\n\nWe are all here for the same humble reason: to reset.\n\nThe machines whir and clunk, and I find myself thinking about cycles in a way I definitely didn't intend at two in the morning. Everything gets dirty. Everything gets clean. Everything gets dirty again. For years I thought that was a sad fact, like entropy or taxes. My clothes will never stay clean. I'll never stay on top of my emails. The kitchen will never stay tidy for more than, oh, eleven minutes.\n\nBut watching them turn, I think maybe it's not sad at all. It's just proof that we're using things. Living in them. The sweater is pilled because I wore it to walk in the rain. The t-shirt is thin because it's been to concerts and bad dates and long drives and hospital waiting rooms. We're not supposed to keep things pristine forever. We're supposed to wash them and wear them again.\n\nThe washer dings. A very polite, very final ding. I have to haul everything over to the dryer, which will take exactly 45 minutes and cost $3.50 in quarters I will pretend I don't mind spending. The clothes are heavy and warm and smell like towers of clean. I stand there in the peace and noise, holding a basket full of damp, hot fabric, and for a second I am disproportionately proud of myself.\n\nI have a clean sweater. Life is, at this precise moment, under control.\n\nOf course, by Thursday it will all be dirty again. I'll put the sweater on, spill coffee on it, and be back here at 2 a.m., doing the whole beautiful, boring, necessary rotation one more time.\n\nClean again. For now, that's enough.",
